# quakes.hal — a live 3D globe of the world's earthquakes, in the browser.
# Run it, then open http://localhost:8080

Serve a web page on port 8080. The server must listen on all interfaces
(0.0.0.0) so it can be reached from outside the container. When it is
ready, print the address to open, http://localhost:8080.

The page is a full-window, dark, space-like scene with a 3D globe in the
middle that slowly spins on its own. The user can drag to rotate it and
scroll to zoom.

Plot every earthquake from the last 24 hours, using the public USGS feed
at https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/feed/v1.0/summary/all_day.geojson.
Each quake is a glowing point that pulses gently. Bigger magnitudes get
bigger, brighter pulses, colored from cool teal for small quakes to hot
red for large ones. Hovering a quake shows its place, magnitude, and how
long ago it happened.

Refresh the data every minute. When a quake appears that wasn't there
before, make it ripple outward across the globe's surface so it's easy to
spot.

In a corner, show a small panel with the number of quakes today, the
strongest one, and when the data last updated.

In the terminal, print one colored line per quake as it arrives: the
time, the magnitude, and the place. Color the line by magnitude the same
way the globe does. Print the whole day's list on startup, then only the
new ones.

Keep running until Ctrl-C, then print how many quakes were seen and exit
cleanly.
